WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.376 --> 00:00:03.027
(upbeat music)

00:00:03.027 --> 00:00:04.860
- [Voiceover] Jeppson's
Malört is considered by a lot

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:06.950
of people to be the most horrible liquor

00:00:06.950 --> 00:00:08.130
they've ever had.

00:00:08.130 --> 00:00:11.210
- To me, Malört tastes like sweaty socks.

00:00:11.210 --> 00:00:12.800
- Metallic pine needles.

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:16.300
- Canteloupe plus tires? Like rubber.

00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:19.153
- Technically, it's like
a digestif, realistically,

00:00:19.153 --> 00:00:22.070
it's sour and regret. It's pretty rough.

00:00:22.070 --> 00:00:24.900
- First thought is awful, but
then it's a acquired taste

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:27.333
that now that I can't,
actually get enough off.

00:00:29.061 --> 00:00:31.588
- (coughs) No.

00:00:31.588 --> 00:00:35.070
(harmonica playing)

00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:38.960
- My name is Tremaine Atkinson.
I am CEO and head distiller

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:40.820
for CH distillery.

00:00:40.820 --> 00:00:44.130
My best description of the taste of

00:00:44.130 --> 00:00:48.260
Jeppson's Malört, is ass, gas, and fire

00:00:48.260 --> 00:00:50.750
all at the same time.
I personally love it,

00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:53.156
and I think it tastes great
but maybe I'm a little weird.

00:00:53.156 --> 00:00:53.989
(chuckles)

00:00:53.989 --> 00:00:56.050
- [Narrator] When a CEO
describes his product

00:00:56.050 --> 00:00:59.420
as something that tastes
like ass, gas, and fire,

00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:01.040
it's safe to assume that there's a story

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:04.280
that needs to be told, and
that story as it turns out,

00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:05.533
is one of legend.

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:09.070
(upbeat music)

00:01:09.070 --> 00:01:11.680
- [Tremaine] Malört was
invented in the 1930s

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:14.980
by Carl Jeppson, a Swedish
immigrant who smoked

00:01:14.980 --> 00:01:17.800
so many cigars that it
killed his taste buds,

00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:19.640
so he had to come up with something with

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:22.100
such a strong flavor
that it could cut through

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:24.360
and give him a real experience.

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:27.160
- [Narrator] So, do tell,
what did he come up with?

00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:29.410
- [Tremaine] We can say that
it does have wormwood in it,

00:01:29.410 --> 00:01:31.770
it, and that is it's primary
flavor; and everything else

00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:33.920
after that is top secret.

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:36.080
- [Narrator] But please,
more about wormwood.

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:39.700
- [Tremaine] Wormwood is a,
a plant; it's really an herb;

00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:42.180
it looks a little bit
just like a twig with

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:45.570
some small leaves on it, and
it grows just about everywhere.

00:01:45.570 --> 00:01:47.900
When you take a bite of it, it is the most

00:01:47.900 --> 00:01:50.730
bitter substance that
you really ever could,

00:01:50.730 --> 00:01:51.563
could bite into.

00:01:51.563 --> 00:01:54.420
- [Narrator] Please excuse
my naiveté, but can someone

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:59.003
please explain why? Why would
anyone drink this stuff?

00:02:01.288 --> 00:02:03.790
- [Tremaine] Jeppson's
Malört is an old school

00:02:03.790 --> 00:02:07.030
Chicago tradition. Chicago
has some really iconic things

00:02:07.030 --> 00:02:10.190
about it, like deep
dish pizza and The Bean

00:02:10.190 --> 00:02:15.190
and Lake Michigan. I think
why Malört is so embraced

00:02:15.330 --> 00:02:18.000
is because those other things are nice,

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:22.030
but Malört is kind of the
badass icon of Chicago;

00:02:22.030 --> 00:02:24.893
it allows us to be ourselves.

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:30.230
I wanted to get involved with
Malört ever since I started

00:02:30.230 --> 00:02:34.900
making booze here in Chicago
because it is such a staple

00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:36.630
of the Chicago drinking scene.

00:02:36.630 --> 00:02:39.220
- [Narrator] Staple?
More like an anti-staple.

00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:41.310
How many shots of this
could you possibly sell?

00:02:41.310 --> 00:02:45.150
- On average, 365 days a
year, people are taking

00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:48.500
10,000 shots of Malört,
so think about that:

00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:51.570
every night 10,000 shots of Malört

00:02:51.570 --> 00:02:53.889
are disappearing into peoples bodies.

00:02:53.889 --> 00:02:54.940
(chuckles)

00:02:54.940 --> 00:02:56.985
That was a really weird way to say that.

00:02:56.985 --> 00:02:58.590
(laughs)

00:02:58.590 --> 00:03:00.763
- [Narrator] Weird indeed, but point made.

00:03:01.690 --> 00:03:05.476
- [Tremaine] I vaguely remember
my first shot of Malört.

00:03:05.476 --> 00:03:06.430
- [Narrator] (groans) Same.

00:03:06.430 --> 00:03:09.867
- I remember my friend
who was from here said,

00:03:09.867 --> 00:03:12.260
"Oh, you've got to try a
shot of this." And I took it,

00:03:12.260 --> 00:03:15.577
and I just said, "Why? Why
did you do that to me?"

00:03:17.010 --> 00:03:20.360
That was how I was introduced
to it, but now I love Malört

00:03:20.360 --> 00:03:24.130
because I love the way it tastes;
I think it is fascinating;

00:03:24.130 --> 00:03:27.880
I love the balance between
the pure bitterness

00:03:27.880 --> 00:03:30.480
and the herbal and floral flavors,

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:34.810
that little touch of sweetness,
the heat of the alcohol,

00:03:34.810 --> 00:03:38.220
all of that adds up to me as
a really, really interesting

00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:41.540
taste experience, and I also
love the way it makes me feel.

00:03:41.540 --> 00:03:43.640
- [Narrator] I love the
way that you love Malört,

00:03:43.640 --> 00:03:47.110
and I only wish that one
day I will be so lucky

00:03:47.110 --> 00:03:50.807
to love something as much as
you love your beloved Malört.

